DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       AAmmqq  provides a simple way of determining the current state of aammdd pro‐
       gram.  Communication is by RPC.  Three modes of operation are supported
       by the current protocol.  By default a list of mount points  and  auto‐
       mounted  filesystems  is  output.  An alternative host can be specified
       using the _-_h option.

       If _d_i_r_e_c_t_o_r_y names are given, as output by default, then per‐filesystem
       information is displayed.

OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       --ff     Ask the automounter to flush the internal caches and reload  all
              the maps.


       --hh _h_o_s_t_n_a_m_e
              Specify  an  alternate host to query.  By default the local host
              is used.  In an HP‐UX cluster, the root server is queried by de‐
              fault, since that is the system on which the automounter is nor‐
              mally run.


       --ii     Print information about the mount maps.


       --ll _l_o_g___f_i_l_e
              Tell amd to use _l_o_g___f_i_l_e as the log  file  name.   For  security
              reasons,  this  must  be  the  same log file which amd used when
              started.  This option is therefore only useful to refresh  amd’s
              open  file handle on the log file, so that it can be rotated and
              compressed via daily cron jobs.


       --mm     Ask the automounter to provide a list  of  mounted  filesystems,
              including  the  number  of references to each filesystem and any
              error which occurred while mounting.


       --pp     Return the process ID of the  remote  or  locally  running  amd.
              Useful  when you need to send a signal to the local amd process,
              and would rather not have to search through the  process  table.
              This option is used in the _c_t_l_‐_a_m_d script.


       --qq     Suppress error messages produced when attempting synchronous un‐
              mounts with the --uu option.


       --ss     Ask the automounter to provide system‐wide mount statistics.


       --uu     Ask  the  automounter to unmount the filesystems named in _d_i_r_e_c_‐
              _t_o_r_y instead of providing information about them.  Unmounts  are
              requested, not forced.  They merely cause the mounted filesystem
              to timeout, which will be picked up by aammdd’s main scheduler thus
              causing the normal timeout action to be taken.  If the --uu option
              is  repeated,  aammqq  will attempt to unmount the file system syn‐
              chronously by waiting until the timeout action is taken and  re‐
              turning  an error if the unmount fails.  Any error messages pro‐
              duced may be suppressed with the --qq option.


       --vv     Ask the automounter for its version information.  This is a sub‐
              set of the information output by aammdd’s _‐_v option.


       --ww     Translate a full pathname as returned by _g_e_t_c_w_d(3) into a  short
              AAmmdd  pathname  that goes through its mount points.   This option
              requires that AAmmdd is running.


       --xx _l_o_g___o_p_t_i_o_n_s
              Ask the automounter to use  the  logging  options  specified  in
              _l_o_g___o_p_t_i_o_n_s  from now on.  Note that the "fatal" and "error" op‐
              tions cannot be turned off.


       --DD _d_e_b_u_g___o_p_t_i_o_n_s
              Ask the automounter to use the debugging  options  specified  in
              _d_e_b_u_g___o_p_t_i_o_n_s from now on.


       --HH     Display short usage message.


       --PP _p_r_o_g_r_a_m___n_u_m_b_e_r
              Contact an alternate running amd that had registered itself on a
              different  RPC  _p_r_o_g_r_a_m___n_u_m_b_e_r and apply all other operations to
              that instance of the automounter.  This is useful when  you  run
              multiple  copies of amd, and need to manage each one separately.
              If not specified, amq will use the default  program  number  for
              amd,  300019.   For security reasons, the only alternate program
              numbers amd can use range from 300019 to 300029, inclusive.


       --TT     Contact aammdd using the TCP transport only.  Normally aammqq will try
              TCP, and if that failed, will try UDP.


       --UU     Contact aammdd using UDP (connectionless) transport only.  Normally
              aammqq will try TCP, and if that failed, will try UDP.


